### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### TOKEN REVOCATION — DELETE /auth/tokens/:jti
###
### Every token minted by /auth/delegate contains a unique `jti` (JWT ID).
### If a scoped token is leaked or needs to be invalidated before it expires,
### an admin token can revoke it immediately via DELETE /auth/tokens/:jti.
###
### How to find the jti:
###   1. Copy the token from the /auth/delegate response.
###   2. Paste it into http://jwt.io — the decoded payload shows the `jti` field.
###   3. Or decode manually: take the middle segment (between the two dots)
###      and base64-decode it. The `jti` is a UUID, e.g. "a1b2c3d4-...".
###
###   ⚠️  The :jti path parameter must be the UUID string from the payload,
###       NOT the raw base64 segment of the token.
###
###   Example decoded payload:
###     { "sub": "temp-client", "exp": 1777562878, "scopes": [...], "jti": "40017835-bf5a-41b8-a492-6bfd124f1d27" }
###   Correct revoke URL:
###     DELETE /auth/tokens/40017835-bf5a-41b8-a492-6bfd124f1d27   ✅
###   Wrong (do NOT use the base64 segment):
###     DELETE /auth/tokens/eyJzdWIiOiJ0ZW1wLWNsaWVudC...          ❌
###
### After revocation:
###   Any request using the revoked token returns 401 Unauthorized.
###   The revocation entry is pruned automatically once the token's TTL expires.
###
### Only admin (*:*:*) tokens can revoke tokens.
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Step 1 — Mint a short-lived scoped token to demonstrate revocation
### Run this request, copy the token from the response, then:
###   - Paste it into http://jwt.io to read the `jti` UUID from the payload.
###   - Set @revoke_token below to the full token string.
###   - Set @revoke_jti below to the UUID value of the `jti` field.
POST http://localhost:1538/auth/delegate
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "client_id": "temp-client",
  "scopes": ["read:laptops:*"],
  "ttl_secs": 3600
}

###

### Paste the token from Step 1 here:
@revoke_token = eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJ0ZW1wLWNsaWVudCIsImV4cCI6MTc3NzU2ODY4Miwic2NvcGVzIjpbInJlYWQ6bGFwdG9wczoqIl0sImp0aSI6ImZkMWNjNWMzLTgzYTQtNDQzZS04NmU1LWQzZTczNmJmMjViZiJ9.7TvdjVFHO-h1KrtILoBiBA_AMrVGL-b0QvtLncJsbAQ
### Paste only the jti UUID from the decoded payload here (NOT the base64 segment):
@revoke_jti = fd1cc5c3-83a4-443e-86e5-d3e736bf25bf

###

### Step 2 — Confirm the token works before revocation
### ✅ Should return 200 OK
GET http://localhost:1538/collections/laptops/docs/lp1
Authorization: Bearer {{revoke_token}}

###

### Step 3 — Revoke the token by its jti UUID
### The `exp` field (Unix timestamp from the decoded payload) sets the prune deadline —
### the revocation entry is automatically cleaned up after the token would have expired.
### ✅ Returns: { "revoked": "<jti>", "message": "Token has been revoked successfully" }
DELETE http://localhost:1538/auth/tokens/{{revoke_jti}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "exp": 9999999999
}

###

### Step 4 — Confirm the token is now rejected
### ❌ Should return 401 Unauthorized: "Token has been revoked"
### Uses the same token as Step 2 — same jti, now in the revocation store.
GET http://localhost:1538/collections/laptops/docs/lp1
Authorization: Bearer {{revoke_token}}

###

### ❌ Non-admin token cannot revoke — 403 Forbidden
### Mint a read-only token first (§A2 above), then try to use it to revoke.
DELETE http://localhost:1538/auth/tokens/some-jti
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{col_token}}

{
  "exp": 9999999999
}

###


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### TELEMETRY
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ Health check — publicly accessible, no auth required
### Returns: { "status": "ok", "message": "MoltenDB is running" }
GET http://localhost:1538/system/health

###

### ✅ Metrics — admin only
### Returns host RAM totals, free/used, process RAM, and per-disk usage.
### ❌ Returns 403 Forbidden if called with a non-admin token.
GET http://localhost:1538/system/metrics
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

###

### ❌ Non-admin token cannot access metrics — 403 Forbidden
GET http://localhost:1538/system/metrics
Authorization: Bearer {{col_token}}


### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### WEBSOCKET — REAL-TIME PUSH & PERMISSION TESTING
###
### MoltenDB WebSocket endpoint: ws://localhost:1538/ws  (or wss:// in prod)
###
### Protocol summary:
###   1. Connect to /ws (no auth header — auth happens inside the socket).
###   2. Send as the FIRST message:
###        { "action": "AUTH", "token": "<jwt>" }
###      ✅ Valid, non-revoked token  → server replies:
###        { "status": "authenticated", "message": "..." }
###      ❌ Invalid / revoked token   → server replies with an error and closes.
###   3. After auth, the server pushes change events for every write on a
###      collection the token's scopes cover:
###        { "event": "change", "collection": "<name>", "key": "<key>", "new_v": <v> }
###      Events for collections outside the token's scopes are silently dropped
###      server-side — the client never sees them.
###   4. All CRUD must go through the HTTP endpoints. WebSockets are read-only
###      push notifications.
###
### HOW TO TEST WITH A WEBSOCKET CLIENT
###   Use any WS client: websocat (CLI), Postman, Insomnia, browser DevTools, etc.
###
###   websocat example (install: cargo install websocat):
###     websocat ws://localhost:1538/ws
###     > {"action":"AUTH","token":"<paste token here>"}
###     < {"status":"authenticated","message":"..."}
###     (now trigger a write via HTTP in another terminal — events appear here)
###
###   Browser DevTools console example:
###     const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:1538/ws");
###     ws.onmessage = e => console.log(JSON.parse(e.data));
###     ws.onopen    = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({action:"AUTH",token:"<token>"}));
###
### The HTTP requests below mint the tokens and trigger the writes you need to
### observe the filtering in action. Run them while your WS client is connected.
### ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


### ── SCENARIO A: Scoped token only receives events for its collection ──────────
###
### Token scope: read:laptops:*
### Expected WS behaviour:
###   ✅ Writes to `laptops`  → event pushed to this client
###   ❌ Writes to `memory`   → event silently dropped (never reaches the client)
###   ❌ Writes to `display`  → event silently dropped

### A0. 🔌 Open the WebSocket connection with the scoped token
###   Run this FIRST, keep it open, then run A1–A4 to observe filtered events.
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

{"action": "AUTH", "token": "{{ws_laptops_token}}"}

###

### A1. Mint a laptops-only read token
POST http://localhost:1538/auth/delegate
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "client_id": "ws-laptops-watcher",
  "scopes": ["read:laptops:*"],
  "ttl_secs": 3600
}

###

@ws_laptops_token = eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJ3cy1sYXB0b3BzLXdhdGNoZXIiLCJleHAiOjE3Nzc2NjA5NjcsInNjb3BlcyI6WyJyZWFkOmxhcHRvcHM6KiJdLCJqdGkiOiI5OTI3Y2RlMi01YzUzLTQyNGItYjkwMC0yYTc3NTQyOWJkZGQifQ.w30MaaLRxFE7oSbSpyMpLJJwdg6jBySSyqTxufGF1i0

### A2. ✅ Trigger a write to `laptops` — WS client SHOULD receive the event:
###   { "event": "change", "collection": "laptops", "key": "lp_ws_test", "new_v": 1 }
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "laptops",
  "data": {
    "lp_ws_test": { "brand": "WS Test", "model": "Event Check", "price": 1, "in_stock": true }
  }
}

###

### A3. ❌ Trigger a write to `memory` — WS client should NOT receive anything
###   (the server drops the event before sending — no message arrives on the socket)
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "memory",
  "data": {
    "mem_ws_test": { "capacity_gb": 8, "type": "DDR5", "speed_mhz": 5600, "upgradeable": true }
  }
}

###

### A4. Cleanup — remove the test documents
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "laptops",
  "key": "lp_ws_test"
}

###

POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "memory",
  "key": "mem_ws_test"
}

###


### ── SCENARIO B: Admin token receives events for ALL collections ──────────────
###
### Token scope: *:*:*
### Expected WS behaviour:
###   ✅ Writes to ANY collection → event pushed to this client

### B1. Use the existing @admin_token (minted in LEVEL 6 above) or mint a fresh one:
POST http://localhost:1538/auth/delegate
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "client_id": "ws-admin-watcher",
  "scopes": ["*:*:*"],
  "ttl_secs": 3600
}

###

@ws_admin_token = PASTE_TOKEN_HERE

### B2. ✅ Write to `laptops` — admin WS client receives the event
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "laptops",
  "data": {
    "lp_admin_ws": { "brand": "Admin WS", "model": "All Collections", "price": 1, "in_stock": true }
  }
}

###

### B0. 🔌 Open the WebSocket connection with the admin token
###   Run this FIRST, keep it open, then run B1–B4 to observe all-collection events.
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

{"action": "AUTH", "token": "{{ws_admin_token}}"}

###

### B3. ✅ Write to `display` — admin WS client ALSO receives this event
###   (the laptops-only token from Scenario A would NOT receive this)
POST http://localhost:1538/set
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "display",
  "data": {
    "dsp_admin_ws": { "size_inch": 27.0, "resolution": "3840x2160", "panel": "IPS", "refresh_hz": 144, "touchscreen": false, "hdr": true }
  }
}

###

### B4. Cleanup
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "laptops",
  "key": "lp_admin_ws"
}

###

POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "display",
  "key": "dsp_admin_ws"
}

###


### ── SCENARIO C: Revoked token is rejected on WS connect ─────────────────────
###
### A token that has been revoked via DELETE /auth/tokens/:jti must be rejected
### at the WebSocket AUTH step — the connection is closed immediately.
###
### Steps:
###   1. Mint a token (C1).
###   2. Confirm it works on a normal HTTP endpoint (C2).
###   3. Revoke it (C3).
###   4. Try to open a WS connection with it — server must reject it (C4).

### C1. Mint a short-lived token to revoke
POST http://localhost:1538/auth/delegate
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "client_id": "ws-revoke-test",
  "scopes": ["read:laptops:*"],
  "ttl_secs": 3600
}

###

### Paste the token and its jti (from jwt.io or the response) here:
@ws_revoke_token = eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJ3cy1yZXZva2UtdGVzdCIsImV4cCI6MTc3NzY2MTkxNywic2NvcGVzIjpbInJlYWQ6bGFwdG9wczoqIl0sImp0aSI6ImIyMjYwNGQ1LWE1YTYtNDkzYi05MzRhLTlhYjViMTZhOTUyNyJ9.ghTt4VigeW7FI8OIuX8glnPOro7aElPdnOMo6sxHXr0
@ws_revoke_jti   = b22604d5-a5a6-493b-934a-9ab5b16a9527

### C2. ✅ Confirm the token works before revocation
GET http://localhost:1538/collections/laptops/docs/lp1
Authorization: Bearer {{ws_revoke_token}}

###

### C3. Revoke the token
DELETE http://localhost:1538/auth/tokens/{{ws_revoke_jti}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "exp": 9999999999
}

###

### C4. ❌ Open the WebSocket with the revoked token — server must reject it.
###   Expected: server replies with an error JSON and closes the connection.
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

{"action": "AUTH", "token": "{{ws_revoke_token}}"}

###

### C4b. Verify on HTTP too — should return 401:
GET http://localhost:1538/collections/laptops/docs/lp1
Authorization: Bearer {{ws_revoke_token}}

###


### ── SCENARIO D: No AUTH message → immediate rejection ───────────────────────
###
### If the first WS message is anything other than a valid AUTH frame,
### the server closes the connection immediately.

### D1. ❌ Wrong action — server rejects and closes
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

{"action": "SUBSCRIBE", "collection": "laptops"}

###

### D2. ❌ Missing token field — server rejects and closes
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

{"action": "AUTH"}

###

### D3. ❌ Garbage / plain text — server rejects and closes
WEBSOCKET ws://localhost:1538/ws

hello

###

### In all cases the server replies:
###   { "error": "Authentication required. Send {\"action\":\"AUTH\",\"token\":\"<jwt>\"} as the first message." }
### and closes the socket.

